[{:seon.task/name "a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the printer audit (docs/render.md; owner, 2026-10-25: \"focus on not generating crap and then not rendering crap\"). An entry of ```seon.problems/health``` carries keys that say nothing, required by :seon.problems/row: `:producer nil`, `:at-ms nil`, `:proofs []`, `:answered-by []` on every derived entry (```seon.problems/derived-row```, the entry function in ```seon.problems/unnamed-sites```), and repeats itself: a recorded error's entry (```seon.problems/error-row```) holds :example, :signature, :count and :agents both at its top and in :evidence. Printed raw (a value that lost its schema's metadata, e.g. (:classes (my.root/problems sys))), the 177 :stale-section entries were 13 keys each, most of them empty. Correct: an entry holds a key only when it knows its value (the schema's keys optional, nil meaning unknown never stored), and :evidence only what the entry's own keys do not say; its readers (the done and merge gates, seon.render.ai/problem-data, seon.render.ui/problem-row) read a missing key as none. Proof: every entry of health on a seeded db has no nil or empty value and no :evidence key equal to its own."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows}}
 {:seon.task/name "closed-names-no-legacy-subject"
  :seon.task/plan "The fallback this task named is gone (093ace07cf): `seon.problems/closed?` reads only an error's :seon.error/subject, and a dead citation is refused at its write, so no :seon.doc/refused error is recorded any more. What remains: a test that every recorded error class names a subject at its producer (no such test exists; `seon.problems-test` has only the one that a subject closes an error when it changes), and the standing store's old entries without a subject (at least db 1795, task file my.task.edn not readable EDN, and db 1979, git add -A unable to stat a path), which only the owner's go retracts (one write through seon_eval root) unless runs/store is erased, in which case the test is all."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-measure-past-its-bound-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03: \"If we put in bounds we could have problems detect themselves. The point is to passively update it progressively as the system runs rather than do a poll of the current system\"; and before, on health: \"We are supposed to be passively collecting data and problems\" (agent evals, turn times, new agents). What exists: the measures are recorded as facts at their writes: an eval's :ms :ticks :bytes (in :seon.eval/response, seon.repl/record-tx), a model call's :ms, tokens, cache hit and miss tokens and its split (:seon.model-call/*), an agent's turn (:seon.agent/turn), a task's outcome and waste (:seon.task/outcome, :seon.task/waste, waste only at the end), a process's forked and ended ms and mem-mb (:seon.process/*). Two measures already become a recorded problem at their write: :seon.turn/cache-miss (seon.turn) and :seon.process/stall (seon.problems/stalled-agents). The rest have no bound, so nothing judges them: on the standing root (2026-11-03) one eval took 2.7 GB of bytes and nothing was open. Health (seon.problems/health) lists problems only, no measures; an agent's context (seon.ctx's work view) shows its proofs, the done rule's reasons and the problems its branch caused (seon.problems/caused-split), not its measures against any bound; the model-call facts stay on the agent's branch, so main holds none (0 on the standing root after a green 3-turn run). The change, one mechanism: (1) each bound is a config key in resources/seon/config/default.edn under seon.config.*, set from measured costs (memory: limits fail loudly; no made-up limits): an eval's ticks and bytes, a run's turns and tokens, a model call's cache hit share, a process's mem-mb, an agent's wait; wall time is measured and shown, never judged (docs/cost.md#what-is-measured). (2) The writer of each measure compares it with its bound at the write and records a problem fact holding the measure and the bound, as cache-miss does: one comparison a write, nothing on an idle system, no poll and no proc. Generalize cache-miss and stall into it rather than beside them. (3) Health reads them as a recorded class; an agent's context shows the open ones on its own branch on its next turn; root's render shows all. (4) Aggregates (turns to green, agents started, tokens a task, the distribution of eval cost) are a render over the facts, drawn on read at the cost of what changed since its t (derived data cached by t, never relied on), for the dashboard and a root agent's context. (5) The run's measures reach main: the model-call facts (or their per-run sums) merge with the run, so main's aggregates see every run. Measure first: the bounds come from the distributions on a run of real tasks. Docs: docs/health.md (a measure past its bound), docs/cost.md, docs/dashboard.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/an-eval-past-its-bytes-bound-opens-a-problem seon.problems-test/a-runs-measures-reach-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-names-change"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-05: \"Name changes too. I don't want these old ideas to come back and haunt us again\"; keep the docs and tasks updated rather than a list of what not to say. And (owner, 2026-11-06): \"yeah I always hated the text name. we need better names in general. Let's include that with the context changes so we are all on the same page and don't resurrect unclear naming\". In one merge after the code they name is settled, now that the components answer hiccup and diffs are in; it renames src/seon/render/ui.clj, so whoever is editing that file hears first. \"Text\" means three things and each gets its own name: an agent's words are ``intent`` per eval and the reply's text, its message, per turn, already so on the wire and in the db (``:seon.eval/intent``, ``:seon.model-call/reply``), so its uses in the chat and the wiki go; the printer's mark for text shown as itself against code, seon.print/plain and :seon.print/plain, becomes ``markdown``; CommonMark's sense in seon.ref's reader becomes ``text``, CommonMark's own word; the CSS class seon-text becomes ``seon-md``, beside seon-md-inline. \"Look\" is a schema's ``:seon.render/hiccup`` function. Component for part, unit and tile, :seon.render/parts included (a stored keyword: the store is erased on the owner's go); ``agent-view`` for render-ctx; ``agent-messages`` for the agent's model render; render tree for walk and walker; ``conversation``, ``turn``, ``eval-record``, ``message``, ``task``, ``cite``, ``turn-line``, ``problems``, ``proofs`` for conversation-list, chat-turn, eval-item and evaluation, chat-message, task-data, cited, run-head, own-problems and status; ``start-work`` for begin-run!; element ids by what they hold (c-rows, p-task, p-tiles, p-head, c-side); test names and :as :row, :rows, fill-rows, form-row and the like with them. Sweeps the 160 of 402 open tasks written on what has gone: rewritten as things now are, or removed when what they plan is gone. Removes jolt/render-core, jolt/insert-and-run, jolt/one-render-6-the-page-is-the-last-message and the worktrees of finished experiments once their good work has merged (a store is the owner's to reclaim). Proofs: bin/prove green; every doc and open task reads in today's names."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ctx/teaching-is-data"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "schema-entries-and-tests-cite-their-section"
